1
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:10,140
Nurse: That's all the plasma we can take
this week. Come back Thursday.

2
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:14,220
Broker: You fell asleep for ten seconds,
and you earned a coin.

3
00:00:15,267 --> 00:00:19,067
Broker: Wear this tonight. Sleep all
night, and get paid for every hour.

4
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:24,533
Twice in seven days, you sell your plasma,
the pale part of your blood.

5
00:00:24,553 --> 00:00:26,013
It's the most US law allows.

6
00:00:27,567 --> 00:00:29,807
It's how your family keeps up with what it
owes.

7
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:33,687
So what if a company could rent your brain
while you slept?

8
00:00:34,633 --> 00:00:37,333
That's the band. A thin strip you wear
across your forehead.

9
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:43,660
While you sleep, it lends your brain to a
company. And it pays you, by the hour.

10
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,020
Midnight. The band goes on. A chime.

11
00:00:47,967 --> 00:00:52,127
That pale figure up there? That's your
sleeping brain, at work for the company.

12
00:00:56,667 --> 00:01:00,387
You feel nothing. But a night you sell is
a night your own brain doesn't get.

13
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:02,833
Brother: What'd you get?

14
00:01:03,133 --> 00:01:04,133
Brother: Scam.

15
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:08,720
Ninety dollars. For eight hours of lying
down. The best wage of your life.

16
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:10,420
And that's the problem.

17
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:14,213
Because you count every waking hour as
money you did or didn't earn.

18
00:01:14,233 --> 00:01:16,713
And nobody's ever paid you for one asleep.

19
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:24,040
The band came with a leaflet. It sets out
the deal.

20
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,440
Wear the band to bed. While you sleep, the
company runs its work on your brain.

21
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:34,020
And every morning, the band pays you, by
the hour.

22
00:01:35,567 --> 00:01:40,307
You won't feel a thing. One company can
link many bands, for one big job.

23
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:46,193
But the leaflet promises each brain stays
separate. One brain, one band, one jar.

24
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:51,613
And you won't remember the work. Only
small leftovers.

25
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:56,073
The leaflet calls them crumbs: a taste, a
word, a face that isn't yours.

26
00:01:57,467 --> 00:01:58,467
It says they're harmless.

27
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:04,080
Why would anyone want a sleeping brain?
The leaflet explains that too.

28
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,100
Your brain burns about twenty watts, a dim
bulb.

29
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:10,240
At night, the leaflet calls that unused
power.

30
00:02:11,733 --> 00:02:13,813
So why doesn't the company just use a
computer?

31
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:18,873
The leaflet has one line on that.
Computers cost money to run.

32
00:02:18,893 --> 00:02:20,713
You're already lying there.

33
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:22,600
Brother: Still a scam.

34
00:02:23,233 --> 00:02:26,853
He reads the leaflet upside down. And by
Saturday he's wearing one.

35
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:33,140
Morning two. You wake craving a food you
can't name. So you draw it for him.

36
00:02:36,667 --> 00:02:37,667
Brother: That's a shoe.

37
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:41,420
Morning three. You hum a tune in the
shower that nobody has written.

38
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:44,460
The grandmother next door bangs on the
wall.

39
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,560
Then she hums it back. Note for note.
Through the wall.

40
00:02:51,533 --> 00:02:54,913
Morning four. You fold a fitted sheet into
a perfect square.

41
00:02:55,453 --> 00:02:58,433
Nobody can fold a fitted sheet. His spoon
hits the floor.

42
00:02:59,333 --> 00:03:00,833
Brother: Since when can you do that?

43
00:03:01,833 --> 00:03:04,093
And the chair? You still sit in it. Why
wouldn't you?

44
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:05,960
Three notes from the chair.

45
00:03:06,967 --> 00:03:09,867
Ninety from the band. Nobody said you had
to choose.

46
00:03:11,067 --> 00:03:12,727
Nurse: You look rested. For once.

47
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:18,260
Morning five. You know the word for bridge
in a language you've never heard.

48
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:19,840
So you try it on the woman at the bridge,

49
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:22,760
the one who crosses twice a week to sell
her plasma too.

50
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:25,040
Bridge woman: Not mine.

51
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:28,800
She shrugs. She doesn't know it either. So
whose is it?

52
00:03:29,667 --> 00:03:34,127
Saturday. Your brother, palm out. And two
teal lights under his cap.

53
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,200
Brother: Okay. What'd you get?

54
00:03:37,267 --> 00:03:39,467
Two bands. Because why not wear two?

55
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:44,520
One brain, one band, the leaflet said.

56
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:47,740
The second band found his brain busy and
paid nothing.

57
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,460
He slept in a hat with two lights on it.

58
00:03:50,233 --> 00:03:51,233
Brother: Worth a shot.

59
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:56,633
Breakfast becomes a lottery draw. His
crumb, your crumb, in his notebook.

60
00:03:56,973 --> 00:04:00,573
A taste, a tune, a word, a face. He wakes
knowing a knot nobody taught him.

61
00:04:00,873 --> 00:04:02,973
You wake saying sorry in a voice that
isn't yours.

62
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:06,013
So is a sleeping brain really working?

63
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:10,493
About a hundred years ago a scientist said
the experiment that won him a Nobel Prize

64
00:04:10,513 --> 00:04:13,653
came to him in a dream. He got up in the
night and did it, on a frog's heart.

65
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,820
Sleeping brains do real work. Normally,
you keep it.

66
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:23,400
But here's the problem. Problem one.
Ninety dollars for eight hours.

67
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:24,800
So what's twelve worth?

68
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:27,180
You rent your nap on the bus.

69
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,680
You wake at the end of the line with four
dollars and a craving for pickles.

70
00:04:31,833 --> 00:04:33,633
And you're not the only one counting.

71
00:04:33,653 --> 00:04:37,253
The nurse has a band under her cap now, so
the clinic shuts at noon.

72
00:04:37,993 --> 00:04:42,113
Therefore your boss sees the jar on your
desk, and opens a nap room at work.

73
00:04:42,133 --> 00:04:43,133
With a till.

74
00:04:43,567 --> 00:04:45,707
Boss: Twenty per cent. Pillows are mine.

75
00:04:46,633 --> 00:04:48,853
Therefore sleeping for money is a day job.

76
00:04:49,373 --> 00:04:53,293
So the couple across the street sleep in
shifts, and their jar never stops.

77
00:04:53,933 --> 00:04:56,333
And the empty warehouse by the bridge
fills with cots,

78
00:04:56,353 --> 00:04:58,713
and a clerk with a clipboard stands at its
door.

79
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,660
Therefore the grandmother next door,
eleven hours a night,

80
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:04,360
out-earns the whole street.

81
00:05:05,233 --> 00:05:06,233
Grandmother: Finally.

82
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:11,280
Therefore nobody buys coffee. The coffee
shops close.

83
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:15,180
The pillow shop stays open till midnight.
And the whole city is in bed by seven.

84
00:05:20,667 --> 00:05:23,447
It makes the evening news. Nobody's up to
watch it.

85
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:29,320
Eleven hours. So why not all day? You try.
Noon, the bunk, the band on.

86
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:37,180
Your brain won't go. The pale figure sits
over you, arms folded.

87
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:41,360
The band pays for sleep, not for lying
there with your eyes shut.

88
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:43,200
Brother: You're doing it wrong.

89
00:05:44,133 --> 00:05:45,673
So you push the nights instead.

90
00:05:46,733 --> 00:05:50,873
Ten hours a night. Then twelve. Your jar
becomes a shelf of jars.

91
00:05:51,933 --> 00:05:54,893
So what did twelve hours cost? First your
keys.

92
00:05:55,233 --> 00:05:59,673
Then your PIN, gone, at the cash machine.
Then you walk past your own front door.

93
00:05:59,693 --> 00:06:00,693
Twice.

94
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:06,300
Brother: You live here.

95
00:06:06,667 --> 00:06:10,047
You sold twelve hours of nothing. And the
nothing was never empty.

96
00:06:10,547 --> 00:06:11,947
Sleep was never idle.

97
00:06:12,833 --> 00:06:16,053
You thought you were selling spare time.
There was no spare time.

98
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:19,820
You spend about a third of your life
asleep. A third.

99
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:21,660
Did you think it was for nothing?

100
00:06:23,333 --> 00:06:23,553
In mice,

101
00:06:23,573 --> 00:06:28,313
the spaces between brain cells open up by
about sixty per cent during sleep,

102
00:06:28,333 --> 00:06:30,513
and the waste washes out faster.

103
00:06:30,533 --> 00:06:33,853
If yours does the same, sleep is when your
brain takes the trash out.

104
00:06:34,493 --> 00:06:35,493
Yours hasn't in a month.

105
00:06:36,233 --> 00:06:37,233
Brother: Explains the smell.

106
00:06:38,233 --> 00:06:42,293
In rats, the day's activity replays in
sleep, faster than real time.

107
00:06:43,167 --> 00:06:45,567
If yours does the same, it files your day
at night.

108
00:06:45,587 --> 00:06:48,347
And for a month, nothing's been filed.

109
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:53,860
Sleep was never nothing. It was the one
shift you always worked for yourself.

110
00:06:55,300 --> 00:06:57,660
Your brother stops laughing. He gets out
the stamp.

111
00:07:02,167 --> 00:07:03,167
Brother: Don't peel it.

112
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:08,000
But here's the second problem. Problem
two. It's your brain, only yours.

113
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:09,840
Whatever you lose, you lose alone.

114
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:12,800
And Saturday is his graduation.

115
00:07:13,867 --> 00:07:16,887
Saturday. The hall. Folding chairs.

116
00:07:16,907 --> 00:07:20,927
Your brother on the stage in a gown and
his cap, and no band under it today.

117
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:29,240
You read the small print nobody else on
the street has. Weekend nights pay triple.

118
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:31,880
So that night, you take twelve hours.

119
00:07:32,867 --> 00:07:34,027
Brother: What'd you get? Show me.

120
00:07:34,900 --> 00:07:39,140
Two hundred and seventy dollars. The best
night yet. You show him the band.

121
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:40,160
He shows you his phone.

122
00:07:43,567 --> 00:07:44,847
Brother: You cried. Remember?

123
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:50,600
Brother: Fine. I'll fail something. You
can come to the retake.

124
00:07:51,633 --> 00:07:52,993
You were there. You have the photo.

125
00:07:53,013 --> 00:07:56,053
You sold the night before your brain could
file the day.

126
00:07:56,900 --> 00:07:59,620
But here's the third problem. Problem
three.

127
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:02,500
You weren't the only one who worked out
the weekend rate.

128
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:03,680
And the price is about to fall.

129
00:08:05,033 --> 00:08:08,773
Ninety. Forty-one. Twelve. Everyone has a
band on now.

130
00:08:09,633 --> 00:08:09,793
It's true.

131
00:08:09,813 --> 00:08:13,753
The US is one of the few countries that
lets companies pay people for plasma.

132
00:08:14,567 --> 00:08:17,027
It supplies about two thirds of the
world's plasma.

133
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:22,880
People cross that bridge from Mexico to
sell theirs, some twice a week.

134
00:08:24,233 --> 00:08:28,913
In 2019, a typical worker across the river
made about nine dollars a day.

135
00:08:28,933 --> 00:08:29,933
Now they cross to sleep.

136
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:35,800
By week six, the border has a rule for it.
No sleeping on a visitor visa.

137
00:08:36,767 --> 00:08:39,227
The three men in grey suits stand right
under the sign.

138
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:40,900
The warehouse by the bridge.

139
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:45,820
A thousand sleepers in shifts, a thousand
pale workers typing over them.

140
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:50,680
An eighteenth-century chess robot had a
chess master hidden inside it.

141
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:54,360
Amazon named a website after it,
Mechanical Turk,

142
00:08:54,380 --> 00:08:59,040
and paid people a few cents a task. This
is the human inside the machine. In bulk.

143
00:08:59,967 --> 00:09:02,147
And the best-rested brains fetch the most.

144
00:09:02,747 --> 00:09:06,307
More than a third of American adults
already don't get enough sleep.

145
00:09:06,327 --> 00:09:08,367
So the people who need the money most earn
the least.

146
00:09:09,367 --> 00:09:11,967
Your brother, band on, at the warehouse
door at midnight.

147
00:09:12,967 --> 00:09:13,987
Brother: I'm extremely rested.

148
00:09:18,633 --> 00:09:19,633
Clerk: Rested ones only.

149
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:25,000
So a night is worth twelve dollars, and he
can't even sell that.

150
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:29,320
But one offer hasn't fallen. The man in
the grey suit comes back.

151
00:09:30,133 --> 00:09:33,233
The kitchen table. A premium contract.

152
00:09:33,253 --> 00:09:36,253
Triple pay, every night, all night, for a
year.

153
00:09:36,273 --> 00:09:40,033
It clears what the family owes by spring.
And no more Thursdays in the chair.

154
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:43,220
Sign, and nobody in this house sits in
that chair again.

155
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:48,700
Don't, and it's Thursday forever. You
can't sleep on it. Sleeping is the job.

156
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:50,700
You pick up the pen.

157
00:09:53,967 --> 00:09:55,087
Brother: Not yet. Look.

158
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:02,480
Six weeks of crumbs, his and yours. He
thinks they add up to something.

159
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:04,940
Broker: Is that a shoe?

160
00:10:06,067 --> 00:10:07,067
Broker: Offer closes midnight.

161
00:10:08,167 --> 00:10:09,567
The kitchen clock. Eleven.

162
00:10:10,633 --> 00:10:13,093
The crumbs on the bed. His and yours.

163
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:24,200
The same face. Drawn badly, the same way.

164
00:10:25,067 --> 00:10:26,067
He opens the laptop.

165
00:10:29,567 --> 00:10:33,887
One brain, one band, one jar, the leaflet
said. Each brain stays its own.

166
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:37,900
There was never one of anything. You never
lost anything alone.

167
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:41,120
For eight hours a night, every sleeper on
the band was one mind.

168
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:47,320
In 2020, over a million people lent their
idle home computers to one project.

169
00:10:47,967 --> 00:10:51,727
The scientists behind it say it became the
first computer to pass a billion billion

170
00:10:51,747 --> 00:10:52,747
sums a second.

171
00:10:53,967 --> 00:10:57,067
Spare machines, linked into one. Now spare
brains.

172
00:11:07,267 --> 00:11:08,827
Brother: So what did we get?

173
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:14,140
So what's the job? The crumbs fit
together. Into a drawing.

174
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:15,820
And the drawing is a machine.

175
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:20,640
Why didn't the company just use a
computer? Nobody's built one that can.

176
00:11:21,180 --> 00:11:22,680
Yet. That's the job.

177
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,980
The job the sleepers are paid for is to
design the thing that will never need

178
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:28,000
sleepers.

179
00:11:29,333 --> 00:11:33,333
The biggest idea people ever had together,
and nobody was awake for it.

180
00:11:34,233 --> 00:11:39,813
Eleven fifty-eight. The pen in one hand.
The band in the other, its teal light on.

181
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:43,940
You remember nothing of your nights. Only
the crumbs.

182
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:44,860
And now you know what they were building.

183
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:48,860
You'd rather sit in that chair every
Thursday than build it every night.

184
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,120
Brother: Scam. Told you.

185
00:11:57,733 --> 00:11:58,733
Brother: Come back Thursday.

186
00:11:59,433 --> 00:12:03,253
The man in the grey suit, at midnight, for
your answer. Nobody opens.

187
00:12:04,267 --> 00:12:07,167
You lie down with nothing on your
forehead. The offer's gone.

188
00:12:07,767 --> 00:12:09,847
Yours is the only window on the street not
working.

189
00:12:10,733 --> 00:12:13,393
And for the first time in six weeks,
nobody's typing.

190
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:15,840
You wake. You know your street.

191
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:19,100
You remember yesterday: his face over the
notebook.

192
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,600
And one dream that was only yours. A
bridge, in your own colours.

193
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:25,700
Nobody else dreamt it.

194
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:27,500
Brother: What'd you get, then?

195
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:34,660
Nothing. The best deal on the street, and
you're the only one taking it.

196
00:12:38,067 --> 00:12:41,327
Tonight you'll lie down, and you'll be
clocking in. You always were.

197
00:12:41,867 --> 00:12:42,867
The shift is yours.

198
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:45,560
Thursday. The clinic steps.

199
00:12:46,333 --> 00:12:47,333
Broker: This one's for daytime.

200
00:12:49,833 --> 00:12:51,073
Broker: Everyone else already said yes.

201
00:12:54,633 --> 00:12:56,913
Nobody's asleep. And everybody's typing.

202
00:12:58,500 --> 00:13:01,560
So, would you rent your nights? Tell us in
the comments.

203
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:04,460
And if you'd have done the same, hit like
and subscribe.
